  <static seed="147" source="csafranski/2.7HW_prob9.pg">
    <statement><p>If 
<me>g(x)+ x\sin(g(x))=x^2 - 1 \qquad\text{and}\qquad g(1)=0,</me></p>
<p>then <m>g'(1)=</m> <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\frac{2\cdot 1}{1+1}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>We differentiate both sides.
<me>g'(x)+x\cos(g(x))g'(x)+\sin(g(x))=2x</me>
Solving for g'(x) we get
<me>g'(x)\left[1+x\cos(g(x))\right]=2x-\sin(g(x))</me>
and
<me>g'(x)=\frac{2x-\sin(g(x))}{1+x\cos(g(x))}</me>
Thus 
<me>g'(1)=\frac{2\cdot1-\sin(g(1))}{1+1\cos(g(1))} =\frac{2\cdot1-\sin(0)}{1+1\cos(0)} = \frac{2\cdot1}{1+1}=1</me></p>
</solution>
